  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nA cold northwest flow shortwave will cross over Lake Michigan \nthis evening. Lake enhanced showers are already developing\nacross Michigan and are expected to move into the local forecast\narea in the next few hours. Precipitation will mainly be north\nof the Toll Road and fall as light rain. However, as\ntemperatures drop overnight, some snow could mix in. Winds will\nalso back from northwest to north and then northeast, shifting\nshowers back towards northwest Indiana before they taper off\nTuesday morning. Little to no snow accumulation is forecast\ngiven shallow moisture profiles.\n\nHigh pressure will build back in for Tuesday for dry conditions\nwith highs in the upper 30s to low 40s. The upper level flow\nwill become southerly on Wednesday, and it will warm slightly\ninto the mid 40s. The next weather system will also start moving\nup into the region on Wednesday, bringing rain into the \nsouthwest. Some snow could also mix in on Wednesday night west \nof I-69, but amounts will be minimal. Rain will then overspread \nthe area on Thanksgiving, with a chance for snow showers again \nThursday night into Friday morning. Confidence is low that there\nwill be any accumulations since surface temperatures will \ngenerally remain above freezing. Showers will diminish on Friday\nas the moves into New England. DRier conditions can be expected\nSaturday and Sunday, although a series of weaker shortwaves \ncould bring light precip at times.",
